{
  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Identify the type of data (categorical, discrete, continuous)",
      "Choose the appropriate chart type",
      "For pie charts: calculate angles using (frequency/total) × 360",
      "For scatter: plot points, describe correlation, draw line of best fit",
      "Always label axes and give a title"
    ],
    "title": "How to Represent and Interpret Data",
    "content": "<p><strong>Pie charts:</strong> Each sector angle = \\(\\frac{\\text{frequency}}{\\text{total}} \\times 360°\\). To read: measure the angle and reverse the calculation.</p><p><strong>Scatter graphs:</strong> Show correlation between two variables. Positive (both increase), negative (one up, one down), or no correlation. Draw a <strong>line of best fit</strong> through the middle of the data.</p><p><strong>Bar charts & frequency tables:</strong> Bars for discrete data. Use class intervals for grouped data. <strong>Frequency density</strong> = frequency ÷ class width (for histograms with unequal widths).</p><p><strong>Time series:</strong> Plot data over time. Identify trends (going up/down/staying the same).</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>60 people were surveyed about favourite fruit. 15 chose apple. Find the pie chart angle.</strong></p><p>Angle = \\(\\frac{15}{60} \\times 360 = 90°\\)</p>"
  },
  "topic_links": {
    "prerequisites": []
